#!markdown

# Unknown bits and constraints

`?` represents a bit that may independently be zero or one. Candidate counts
use `BigInteger`; enumeration is always given an explicit upper bound.

#!csharp

#r "nuget: NumeralSystems.Net, 5.3.0"
using NumeralSystems.Net.Type.Incomplete;

var pattern = BitPattern.Parse("1100????");
new {
    Pattern = pattern.ToString(),
    pattern.UnknownBitCount,
    pattern.CandidateCount,
    pattern.MinValue,
    pattern.MaxValue,
    Preview = pattern.EnumerateCandidates(8).ToArray()
}

#!markdown

Compose two constraints. The solver intersects them per bit without enumerating
the candidate space and retains an explanation for every position.

#!csharp

var constraints = BitConstraintSet.Parse(
    "x & 10101010 = 10001000; " +
    "x | 00001111 = 10001111");
var solution = constraints.Solve(new BitConstraintSolverOptions(
    maximumEnumeratedCandidates: 4,
    timeout: TimeSpan.FromSeconds(1)));

new {
    Pattern = solution.GetPatternOrThrow().ToString(),
    solution.CandidateCount,
    Preview = solution.EnumerateCandidates(4).ToArray(),
    Explanation = solution.Explanations
        .Reverse()
        .Select(bit => $"bit {bit.BitIndex}: {bit.Message}")
        .ToArray()
}
